# Pre-upgrade checklist for a macOS major bump. Order matters.

# 1. Dry-run the config on the target OS in a VM first. This proves the flake
#    evaluates and activates on the new major -- it proves NOTHING about
#    whether an in-place upgrade preserves the existing /nix volume.
just -g tart-new tahoe-validate
# then inside the guest: README "macOS setup (Darwin)"

# 2. Back up what nix-darwin and home-manager cannot rebuild.
~/dotfiles/tools/backup/pre-upgrade-backup.sh /Volumes/<external-disk>

# 3. The age identity comes OUT of the archive before encrypting it. An
#    archive encrypted to a key that is only inside that archive is a brick.
#    ~/.age/personal-key.txt (home-manager/modules/git.nix) opens every
#    secret in this repo.

# Rollback vs rebuild -- these are not the same thing:
#   rebuild  = this script + a clone + darwin-rebuild switch
#   rollback = a full Time Machine backup taken BEFORE the upgrade, because
#              Migration Assistant refuses a newer-OS backup onto an older OS,
#              and Apple Silicon has no in-place downgrade: it is erase and
#              reinstall from a bootable installer or DFU.

# Expect to fix after a major bump:
#   - /nix on its own APFS volume (synthetic.conf + fstab + the
#     org.nixos.darwin-store daemon) can come up unmounted. Non-destructive.
#   - homebrew.onActivation.autoUpdate = true means brew updates unattended,
#     and a new OS major changes what its formulae consider supported. See
#     docs/solutions/build-errors/third-party-tap-formula-aborts-darwin-rebuild.md

# Runnable: just -g macos-backup /Volumes/<external-disk>
